{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>You shall not make with Me, etc.</b> One needs to understand the verse's saying the word <b>with Me.</b> And our Sages of blessed memory (Mekhilta) expounded that they should not make four cherubim, and this is the meaning of <b>gods of silver</b> — it holds them accountable as though they made gods of silver — and they should not make the two of silver, and if they did, it holds them accountable, etc. Also, that they should not make a form in the likeness of HaShem to prostrate themselves to it (Rosh HaShanah 24a), and their intent is toward HaShem, not the form of HaShem's attendants. Now, it appears that the verse may be explained with a further fine point: why did He return to say a second time <b>you shall not make for yourselves</b>, and was it not sufficient with what He had previously said, 'You shall not make with Me,' etc.? Further, why did He specify to say the word <b>for yourselves</b>?" "<b>Indeed,</b> HaShem intends to command concerning two types of idolatrous worship: the first is one who worships with faith of his heart, for he believes in it and yearns to worship it, and against this He said <b>You shall not make with Me gods of silver</b> — meaning, gods in silver — and this indicates one who worships out of the faith of his heart. And He specified <b>with Me</b> because one who worships idolatry with his soul's desire draws, into the root of his soul, the aspect of evil that is in the root of that sin — and this is the sod (Eikhah Rabbah 1:31) of 'the serpent that nests in the tower,' for the verse has already informed us that the souls of Israel are the portion of Elohim from above, as it is written (Deuteronomy 32:9) ״חֵלֶק ה׳ עַמּוֹ״, and by worshipping idolatry with the faith of his soul he gives a place for the Samekh-Mem to enter into the place of the root of that soul, which is the place of holiness — and this is what Solomon said (Proverbs 30:22) ״תַּחַת עֶבֶד כִּי יִמְלוֹךְ״ — and this is what He meant by <b>You shall not make with Me</b>. And He said <b>gods of silver</b>, meaning the language of longing (כּוֹסֶף), for he worships it out of his longing for it — may the Merciful One save us." "And the second is one who knows in his heart with complete faith that all of it is nothing and without substance, but he worships it for the sake of benefit, for the enjoyment of gain — as you would say, they give him money on condition that he worship it — and the person says in his heart, 'The Merciful One wants the heart,' while in my heart I revile and blaspheme this idolatry, and my actions are only for the purpose of profit. And against this He said <b>and gods of gold you shall not make for yourselves</b> — meaning, even though his heart and soul do not believe in it at all, but rather because this one loved the gold and for the benefit of his gold he prostrates himself — even so, you shall not make." "And He revealed that this sin is specifically in the aspect of the person himself, that he damages himself and diminishes his image, and this is what He meant by <b>for yourselves</b> — because he sinned only in deed, while the thought is opposed, as is revealed before the One who knows hidden things. Therefore, the sin will not cause the aspect of evil to overpower the aspect of good, as in the first case." "<b>Thus concludes the portion of Yitro.</b>"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:23.619317018Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}